Three flags worth reading twice:

- homie is CC BY-NC. Research and non-commercial use only; a commercial build
  must exclude it. It is fetched rather than committed, because an MIT
  repository cannot sublicense a non-commercial network.

- robomimic and run_residual declare no licence at all, which makes them
  effectively all-rights-reserved. That is why they are fetched rather than
  committed: this repository will not hand you a copy it has no right to hand
  you. The Apache-2.0 LICENSE circulating alongside the RoboMimic weights
  belongs to AMO, a different project.

- amo is Apache-2.0 but sim-only by its authors' explicit request. The upstream
  README warns against third-party hardware deployment, citing a sim-to-real
  failure that damaged a robot.

gr00t_wbc, the candidate that wins the campaign, is NVIDIA Open Model. If
licence terms decide your build, openwbt (Apache-2.0) is the most permissive
candidate with a competitive position error.


2. UNITREE G1 ROBOT DESCRIPTION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The Unitree G1 robot description — assets/g1_29dof.xml and all 36 meshes
under assets/meshes/ — comes from unitree_rl_gym, at
resources/robots/g1_description/, and is redistributed under the BSD 3-Clause
license reproduced below.

  https://github.com/unitreerobotics/unitree_rl_gym

The 36 STL meshes are Modified Versions: every one is decimated to at most a
thousand triangles and 50 KB, which takes the set from 22 MB to 1.7 MB. Twenty
seven of them are collision geometry, and MuJoCo collides a mesh by its convex
hull, so the decimation cuts the hulls from 34,643 vertices to 5,287 and a tour
from 7.8 s to 5.8 s of physics. It also changes contact: the hulls are close to
upstream's but not identical -- torso_link, the heaviest at 44,658 triangles,
loses about 6 mm across a 215 mm span -- so results measured here are not
comparable with results measured against the upstream meshes. Nothing but the
surfaces changed; the kinematics, inertials and joint limits are upstream's.

assets/g1_29dof.xml is a Modified Version: the simulation options and contact
defaults this benchmark runs with were added, joint armature, damping and
friction loss were filled in, and some collision geoms were made invisible.

Unitree Robotics is not affiliated with this benchmark and has not endorsed
it. The name and the logo mesh are theirs.

